00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
hello i'm dilaura di kim  and i'll be talking about my research  project that i conducted with the  omixlogic research fellowship program at  pine biotech  my project was on how radiation is a  threat to humans and space and how this  correlated with the gut brain axis since  they're both hot topics of the century  i'll be discussing why studying the  effects of radiation on the human body  is important then i will explore the  subject  through my own research and findings and  finally link these results  to suggest feasible solutions when the  space inhabitant age of space travel  began two decades ago it brought on a  surge of possibilities in space research  as you can see from the figure several  international space patients conduct  research in various fields  to be able to continue these studies in  outer space the health and well-being of  astro scientists  are of great importance the unusual  conditions present in space stations due  to  increased radiation and microgravity can  cause a myriad of diseases  one of the most prominent conditions  observed in astronauts  who returned to earth after long  durations of space travel  was anxiety and depression  my focus will specifically be on high  lep ionizing radiation  because ionizing radiation causes a  greater threat to human health  than non-ionized radiation since it  carries higher energy  that can cause chemical reactions  whereas it does symmetry for radiation  that can be correlated with biological  effects is lep which is linear energy  transfer  high lead radiation poses a threat  because large structures can easily be  shielded from this type of radiation but  microscopic structures that are either  microbes or human cells cannot  here are some symptoms that are under  study to be controlled or improved upon  by nasa  they have a plan spending the next 10  years on how several implications  experienced in space will be overcome if  research continues at the current speed  radiation exposure is largely  uncontrolled and according to this  scheme it will continue to be  uncontrolled for much longer  given that the dose of radiation  exposure on the iss for six months is  almost 10 times greater than the  abdominal cc scan  there's more than enough reason to worry  to be worried about  its effects on the human body nowadays  research suggests that there's a strong  correlation between microbiome and  psychological health  therefore i suggest that the physical  limitations of space  that have an effect on our psychology  can be overcome by cultivating the  physiological effects of microbes  the course of action i followed to  analyze the effects of radiation on the  mosfet messy genome  subjected to high led ionizing radiation  is as follows  i first obtained the dataset that had 80  samples  categorized in four dosages 0.1.25 and  1gy  and the samples were collected 10 days  and 30 days post radiation  i then use the data 2 pipeline to obtain  abundances of bacteria  present in the sample the complete  dataset was then explored using the  output from the phylloc pipeline  i checked pca and nmds to see the  separation between groups  then i used the shannon index to further  explore the changes in biodiversity  next i checked that different dosages  and days were significantly different  between  groups as observed my aim was to confirm  if the effects of radiation persist even  after exposure is over and if low  dosages  have a greater impact as the  characteristics of ionizing radiation  suggest my first analysis was non-metric  multi-dimensional scaling which is an  iterative algorithm that helps us  visualize the multi-dimensional data in  a two-dimensional state  the data suggests post radiation could  be explored further since they are  grouped more separately  relative to 30 days  next the shannon index was calculated  the shonen index is proportional to the  effects  of number of species in the group which  means that we can reduce the species  richness and evenness using this value  as we can see from this comparison of  all the categories  the index is lower 4.1 and 0.25 gui  therefore moving forward we will focus  on these two groups  and here is an output from the final  seek pipeline where we can observe the  relations between significantly abundant  otu  otu 4 and 5 are related because they're  both in the back of the bacterium family  which is an important  point to keep in mind for the rest of  the research  it can also be observed that for 0.1 and  0.25 gy  at 10 days there is lower abundance  compared to the others  to check if this observable difference  is  significant i followed this with some  statistical tests  in accordance with my previous results i  decided to test 0.1 and 0.25 gy  and compare it to 0gy as my control  i conducted anova and the results  indicated that  in general there was a significant  difference between the three dosages but  not safe  and that there was no interaction  between days and those two to test  further which those which had the most  significant effect i then conducted t  test  and the results displayed a strong trend  in 0.1 gy at 10 days post radiation  being the significant group  i then selected operational taxonomic  units that passed the piece  0.05 threshold and profiled them  my interest was focused on otu 3 4 5  and 8 as you can see from this figure  i also compared the abundances of  different families of bacteria that were  present in  two groups and you can observe a  decrease in erythropoietin  bacteria lactobacillus bacteria and  bifida bacteria  there's also an increase in  varicomicrobia  the results from this graph that align  with my previous findings  from the statistical analysis are  lactobacillus and by  the bacterium they are also associated  with metabolites that have an effect on  the nervous system through the gut brain  axis  the vagus nerve and the enterodendritic  nerve play an important role in this  association connecting the components of  the gut and the  brain and the famous saying your gut is  your second brain  the species corresponding to the ot use  that for significant are  bipolar bacterium pastorius by the  bacterium longum  lactobacillus choline and lactobacillus  causers  they're commonly found in the human gut  too  they're associated with the production  of gaba gamma aminobutyric acid which is  the chief inhibitory trans  neurotransmitter in the central nervous  system  it plays a crucial role in depression  and anxiety  when produced in low levels since these  bacteria have protein choline genes in  the glutamines and glutamine synthase  pathways  we can assume that kava decreases  proportionately to the decrease in the  abundance of these species  since exogenous kava does not have an  effect on the central nervous system  the most efficient method of increasing  gaba is through stimulating its  production by supplying patients with  probiotics  rich and lactobacillus and bipod  bacteria  in conclusion we can sum up these  findings by saying that lower doses of  high let ionizing radiation  has a greater impact on the gut by the  bacterium lactobacillus abundances  more specifically 0.1 gy  the acclimatization period can be  deduced as 10 days  since 30 days post radiation did not  pass a significant threshold  however this rehabilitation period could  be dependent on the length of exposure  the mice in the study were not subjected  to exposure lengths as long as  astronauts on the iss  further studies can include longer  exposure lengths and synergetic effects  of radiation and microgravity  however this was an important precursor  study on how probiotics can be used  in treatment of psychological diseases  and to overcome the effects of radiation  and here are some of the limitations  that i came across while conducting this  research  one of them was optimizing parameters  for the pipeline  the other one was the sample size and  dosages because they were limited  there were only 10 samples for each  category  and there were no dosages for radiation  in between  0.1 and 0.25 and one dose gui  as for the taxonomic profiling for the  16s regions bacteria have very similar  sequences when they're in the same genus  so it was difficult to differentiate  them and the last limitation was that  this study was conducted on mice  which means that we can only make  assumptions on how these mechanisms  truly work in humans  here are my references  i would like to thank the omixlogic  fellowship team for their continuous  support  i had minimal bioinformatics background  before starting this internship and  throughout the three months i acquired  knowledge on various bioinformatics  techniques  from transcriptomics and genomics to  epigenetics and metagenomics  i was able to design and conduct a  project with the guidance of my  supervisors  who supplied me with invaluable feedback  i was very happy  to be part of the scientific community  thank you

